hi there this is steve here i'm now in  palm springs  um  you probably think i do an awful lot of  traveling  which is true  but then again i worked pretty hard for  almost 50 years and i'm not retired so i  can travel  today i want to talk about  motivation  versus interesting or compelling content  now remember if you enjoy these videos  please subscribe by the way i'm not  trying to pretend that i'm incognito  here but the sun is rather bright in my  eyes so i'm wearing these sunglasses so  what triggered this was a post by  stephen grashan stephen krashen whom i  greatly admire  as the clearest and most  i would say to the point explainer of  language acquisition  and he says that motivation is finished  as a factor in language  education  and he would like to replace that with  the idea of interesting or compelling  content  so this caused me to think and i it  caused me to look at my own learning  situation now of course i'm not typical  so the first thing that we have to look  at  in sort of factors that affect success  in language learning is  is the fact that people are all  different  so i am  naturally motivated to learn i want to  learn greek why  just because i visited crete for two  weeks  of course  having assigned myself the task of  learning greek  um  past now my two week or it wasn't really  it was like a 12-day visit to crete i'm  still motivated to learn more and more  because i'm discovering so much about  the culture and i enjoy  being able to read being able to listen  being able to understand being able to  communicate in  greek so i am not typical  the other end of the spectrum  is the classroom where typically most of  the students are not motivated  so i think in the classroom situation  stephen krashen is absolutely right you  cannot browbeat these people the  students by telling them that you need  the language because one day you might  have a career or because you can  discover wonderful cultures none of this  is going to have any impact so for the  student in the classroom  in a high school  you have to give them something that's  compelling some story some movie  something that will you know spark their  interest  however in my case and in the case of  many adults who want to learn a given  language they are motivated so now what  you have to do  is in my view you have to have a sort of  a combination of intrinsically  interesting content  plus some other content that is  beneficial  you know to the learning process but  perhaps not that interesting so in the  case of greek i vary  between  a sort of  interesting content about greek history  about some books while i was in greek on  in greece rather on history and  other content that i know is good for me  like these repetitive mini stories where  i know because the  stories contain a lot of  very common verbs because the vocabulary  repeats so often in each lesson  that i know that repetitive listening to  these stories while it's no longer  interesting is beneficial  uh  similarly i am  dictating these uh texts these are again  call them you know the text part of this  textbook not the grammar exercises or  the um explanations but just that's a  little text that uh in each lesson  there's either one or two of those for  which i have the audio and when i listen  to the audio i don't understand  everything so i'm dictating these so  that i can import them into link this is  not compelling content but it is useful  it is effective learning material  and so  i guess the point i want to make is that  whether you are  you know  driven by motivation  or whether there is an absence of  motivation as is the case in classrooms  uh that will determine what kind of  content is suitable for you  so if you are a relatively unmotivated  person in a classroom or if you are a  teacher teaching these relatively  unmotivated kids you have to find  something that is compelling or  interesting that drives them  to learn the language  if you are an independent learner and  actually quite motivated you still need  to have this interesting and compelling  content because after a while even a  motivated person gets bored dealing with  uninteresting content so i in all of my  language learning i've sought out  interesting content whether it be on the  internet ebooks whatever it might be  but at the same time i do spend  some portion of my time and it'll vary  depending on my mood with essentially  not that interesting content but content  because of which because of the the the  fact that it's repetitive or because i  like the voice of the narrator or  whatever it might be this is content  that my motivation  uh and of course typically this is  content that i will use at an early  stage of learning the language where i  have difficulty accessing more  compelling content  that this kind of content can be very  effective and so ultimately  if you are an independent learner you  get to choose what you want to use as  content you can vary it harder easier  compelling less compelling  whatever your motives are you get to  choose what you learn  in the classroom typically it's the  teacher who decides what the content is  going to be or at least provides  hopefully some choice to the learners  and in that case  i agree with stephen krashen that the  the key there is not necessarily  motivation which might be a futile hope  in the case of many classroom learners  but rather it's a matter of directing  them or guiding those students towards  something that might be interesting to  them and therefore becomes compelling  and therefore drives them to learn the  language and get interested in the  language so just a little chat about the  issue of interest versus motivation  compelling content versus useful content  both in terms of my experience and what  i presume is the experience of a  classroom teacher  thank you for listening bye for now  [Music]  you
 

